# The Mechanics of Litex Proof
#
# This textbook develops mathematical proofs in Litex, inspired by Heather
# Macbeth's The Mechanics of Proof. It progresses from calculation to structured
# proofs, logic, induction, number theory, functions, sets and relations.
#
# Each lesson states its mathematical assumptions, gives a proof idea and shows
# the Litex facts and proof commands that establish the result. Supporting
# results shared across chapters are proved in citation.lit.
#
# When Litex already supplies a familiar concept, the book first introduces its
# mathematical definition and simple examples, then uses the builtin object in
# later calculations. Chapter6 explains the domains and sign conventions for
# quotients, remainders and greatest common divisors.
